### 7. The Outline

---

**H1: Your Data Isn't AI-Ready (And Slapping an Agent on It Won't Help)**

*Target length: 1,200–1,600 words. Audience: CTO, VP Engineering, CDO. Tone: experienced practitioner, direct, no hype.*

---

**Intro** *(~150 words)*
Section notes: Open with the scenario most CTOs recognize — the AI pilot that underdelivered, the agent that hallucinated, the executive who insists "our data is fine." Establish the stakes: bad data processed at AI speed doesn't produce bad results occasionally — it produces bad results continuously, at scale. Frame Nick as a practitioner who has built the systems that work and diagnosed the ones that don't. Thesis: the model is the easy part. Your data is the hard part. And most enterprises are further behind on the data than they think.

---

**H2: What "AI-ready data" actually means — and what it doesn't**

Section notes: Establish Nick's working definition. Pushback on the common assumption that AI-ready means a specific schema, format, or vendor platform. The four dimensions: availability (right speed for the decision), semantic understanding (does the AI know what "customer" means in your organization), governance/security (row-level access applies to agents just like it applies to people), and contextual richness (valid values, labels, ownership).

> H3: Availability isn't just access — it's latency matched to purpose
> - Fraud detection requires milliseconds. End-of-day reporting can wait a day. Data architecture must match the decision it supports.
> - Choosing "tomorrow's good enough" has a cost — so does choosing real-time unnecessarily. The business outcome defines the data requirement.

> H3: Governance means the agent is treated like a person with a job role
> - Row-level security applies to agents. "The AI's harder to trust because you can't see it on an individual basis" — don't give it system-wide access.
> - Prompt injection, data leakage, PII exposure: these aren't theoretical. "Just stand an LLM up against what we've got and feed it everything" is how you expose customer data.
> - The production database failure mode: wide-open agent access + bad data + non-determinism = catastrophic outcomes at machine speed.

`[INSERT DIAGRAM: A layered model showing the four AI-readiness dimensions — availability, semantic context, governance/security, and schema/metadata richness — with examples of what "missing" looks like in each layer]`

---

**H2: How to tell if your data is ready — the new analyst test**

Section notes: Nick's heuristic deserves its own section because it gives readers something they can apply immediately. Build it out fully. Connect it to the absence-of-data hallucination problem.

> H3: What the test reveals
> - If a new analyst couldn't understand your dataset in two weeks, an AI cannot do it in seconds.
> - The "blind customer" example: nothing in the data communicated that external customers have no accounts and cannot store credit cards. A person had to explain it verbally. That explanation doesn't exist for the AI.
> - Semantic gaps — the difference between "member," "customer," "internal customer," "online customer" — produce divergent agent behavior at scale.

> H3: Absent data is as dangerous as wrong data
> - "The absence of data can have just as adverse of effects as having wrong data in there."
> - The medical claims example: a claim without a state label may be adjudicated under the wrong state's rules. The AI didn't have bad data — it had no data, and filled the gap with a confident wrong answer.
> - LLMs are "golden retrievers" — they want to give you an answer. Missing context doesn't produce a refusal; it produces a hallucination.

> H3: What the test doesn't catch
> - Semantic gaps are visible with this test. Latency mismatches, security gaps, and missing ownership aren't. Use this in combination with a governance audit and infrastructure review, not as a standalone pass/fail.

`[INSERT DIAGRAM: A before/after schema comparison — unlabeled, context-free fields vs. enriched schema with labels, valid values, ownership, and SLA — showing what the "new analyst" would encounter in each case]`

---

**H2: The failure modes that AI optimism skips over**

Section notes: The controversy section. Lay out Nick's three counterintuitive arguments clearly and fairly. This is the section most likely to be shared.

> H3: More accuracy at scale means more errors, not fewer
> - 90% accurate on 100,000 daily transactions = 10,000 errors per day. 95% accurate = 5,000. Improving the percentage doesn't reduce the absolute count when volume is growing.
> - The implication: observability and error-handling infrastructure is not optional. You are not eliminating errors — you are managing their rate and catching their effects.

> H3: Agentic AI does the wrong things faster, too
> - "We've talked before about how agents, because they can do things faster, they can do the wrong things faster, too." Bad data + autonomous agent + non-determinism = financial or operational damage at machine speed.
> - The refund scenario: an agent that can approve refunds can incorrectly approve refunds at a rate no human team could achieve. The speed is the risk, not just the benefit.
> - This is why the kill switch and observability are load-bearing, not nice-to-have.

> H3: Agentic AI makes systems easier to build — not necessarily faster to run
> - "I don't know that agentic agents are that much faster or going to speed your business up that much faster. Except it's just easier to produce them and build them."
> - The value proposition is architectural: agents reduce the hand-coded wiring between microservices. They do not reduce the quality requirements on the data those services consume.
> - Developer experience improvement is real. Business velocity improvement is dependent on data readiness — and most enterprises aren't there yet.

---

**H2: What agentic systems actually look like when they work**

Section notes: The dead letter queue story goes here. Show the architecture and the outcome (with a gap flag on missing metrics). Transition into observability requirements.

> H3: The dead letter queue — a real deployment [War story]
> - Setup: 400,000 records stuck, leadership asking why they weren't being resolved
> - Decision: apply an agentic triage process to the queue directly
> - The agent classified each record — system error, customer error, other — and routed it: open a ticket, send an internal alert, or trigger a personalized customer email
> - What made this work: the underlying data had enough structure and semantic context for the agent to make meaningful distinctions. Without that, the agent would triage noise, not signal.
> - *Note: resolution metrics not available — see Gap Analysis. The architecture is sound; the outcome data needs follow-up.*

> H3: Observability is non-negotiable — especially while building trust
> - Every agent action generates an output event. That output event is now data. You need to trace the chain.
> - "As soon as you have something agentic evaluate a message or an event, it's usually generating something else."
> - The transition from human-in-the-loop to autonomous operation is earned through observation, not granted at deployment. While building trust: trace everything, monitor for drift, have a kill switch.
> - Treat agentic systems the same way you treat microservices: same bugs, same edge cases, same need for monitoring and incident response.

