**H2: Your data is the only competitive moat the models can't commoditize**

Section notes: Strategic framing section for the executive audience. The host's photographer story is the strongest example here — attribute it correctly.

> H3: When everyone has the same models, differentiation is entirely in the data
> - "The models are pretty much a commodity. Everybody gets it for $200 a month." Open-source models are less than a year behind frontier models.
> - Building a proprietary model from scratch is a multi-year, multi-million-dollar endeavor that most enterprises can't win. The data those models run on is where advantage lives.
> - "Your data becomes the gas for that engine. That's how you're going to make your differentiator."

> H3: From data warehouse to data product — a shift in relationship
> - Legacy architecture: data sits in tables, queried when needed, owned by nobody in particular.
> - Data product thinking: every dataset has an owner, an SLA, a schema contract, and a defined access mechanism. It is exposable to new use cases without infrastructure rebuilds.
> - When data is a product, a new use case can be stood up in hours — not a quarter. When it isn't, every new use case is a project.

> H3: What "data as moat" looks like in practice
> - The photographer example [Host's story]: a client held exclusive rights to a photographer's work. No public model had been trained on it. Improving built a custom model on that exclusive dataset — now the only way to generate images in that style is through the client's licensed model. A data right became a revenue moat.
> - The broader implication: any dataset that is genuinely proprietary — your transaction history, your operational telemetry, your customer behavior — is a competitive asset. Most enterprises have it and don't know how to expose it.

`[INSERT DIAGRAM: Comparison showing "data as a table" (static, owned by no one, batch-queried) vs. "data as a product" (owner, SLA, schema contract, streaming access) with use case coverage comparison]`

---

**H2: The honest roadmap — where most enterprises actually are**

Section notes: Ground the article in reality. Nick's direct observation: "Most companies are behind." Help the reader see themselves clearly and understand the path.

> H3: The self-assessment most companies fail
> - "When we talk to customers, they are maybe overly generous in their self-assessment of where they are in this journey."
> - Most companies have components of a modern data platform in place. Few have refined them to the point where AI can consume the output without introducing hallucinations or governance failures.
> - The right question isn't "do we have data?" It's "can our data be understood, trusted, and acted on — in seconds — without human context being provided at runtime?"

> H3: The foundation before the agents
> - "I think those are kind of table stakes for having good AI problems." A decade of modern data platform work — ETL, streaming, governance, data products — is the prerequisite, not the alternative, to AI investment.
> - Stacking an AI agent on top of 47 SQL databases and a batch ETL process is not an AI implementation. It is a liability.
> - The right sequence: define business outcomes → determine data requirements → build or refine the foundation → introduce AI where the data is ready to support it.

> H3: Iteration is the mechanism — not planning
> - "You're going to fail about a hundred times first because you don't have it."
> - The internet analogy: two years in, we were building logo websites. We weren't ordering pizza. We're at a similar point with AI — the infrastructure is maturing, the patterns are forming, and most companies are still learning what works.
> - The companies doing this well have failed millions of times. The difference is they have safeguards and can absorb failure safely. Traditional enterprises building production-critical agentic systems without observability don't have that buffer.

---

**H2: The skeptic's case — "Maybe our data really is ready"**

Section notes: This must be a fair steelman, not a strawman. Take the counterargument seriously.

The credible counterargument: Some organizations genuinely are ready. Cloud-native companies built in the last decade — digital-first, with streaming data infrastructure already in place — may have all four readiness dimensions covered. For narrow, low-stakes use cases (document summarization, semantic search, internal Q&A), the data quality bar is lower and the hallucination risk is manageable. The cost of a full modern data platform build-out is real and not always justified by current AI use cases.

Nick's response to this: Even the companies doing it best have failed millions of times. The difference is they have institutional safeguards, experienced teams, and the ability to absorb failures before they reach customers. An enterprise without those safeguards, deploying in production-critical contexts (medical claims, fraud, financial transactions), is taking on a different risk profile. And "low-stakes use cases only" is a valid starting point — but it is not a data strategy.

